Harbor: a browser can show its picture through DirectComposition: one visual, one swap chain, one window (#95).

Chromium's GPU process (WebView2, and with it Office's web add-ins) doesn't present to a window directly. It
makes a DirectComposition device, a target for its window, one visual as the target's root, a swap chain
with IDXGIFactory2::CreateSwapChainForComposition, sets that as the visual's content and commits. Wine had
none of it: DCompositionCreateDevice and CreateSwapChainForComposition returned E_NOTIMPL, the GPU process
kept retrying, and a web view stayed blank. Chromium does this with --disable-direct-composition too.

dcomp: DCompositionCreateDevice returns an IDCompositionDevice with CreateTargetForHwnd, CreateVisual,
IDCompositionTarget::SetRoot, IDCompositionVisual::SetContent and Commit. Nothing is composed. Commit tells
the root visual's swap chain which window it is shown in; everything else (child visuals, offsets,
transforms, clips, effects, animations, surfaces) fails with E_NOTIMPL and a fixme. DCompositionCreateDevice2
and 3 still fail, which makes Chromium fall back to the path above.

dxgi: CreateSwapChainForComposition makes an ordinary swap chain on a hidden window of its own. Once dcomp
has named a window (private data WINE_DXGI_COMPOSITION_TARGET, declared in winedxgi.idl), each present goes
to that window through wined3d's destination-window override. Direct3D 10 and 11 devices only.

Limits: the picture is drawn into the target's window, opaque (premultiplied alpha is treated as "ignore"),
at the window's size. A frame presented before the commit isn't shown again after it. With wined3d's Vulkan
renderer the swap chain stays on the window it was created for, so this works with the OpenGL renderer
only, which is what Office bottles use.

Test: WindowsTests/graphics/childswap.sh (dcomp).

